WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
You will have everything that you need

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:05.160
to let go of your old life by the end of

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:07.240
this video. If you don't believe me,

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:10.120
let's try this together. It only takes

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.360
30 seconds, and for that to work, I want

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.640
you to close your eyes. Now, I know you

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.480
might think, "Let me just watch this

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.760
with my eyes open." But, trust me, if

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.720
you don't close your eyes, this

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.640
experiment won't work. So, close your

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.940
eyes now, and imagine it's 10 years

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.680
later in the future, and your life

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:27.840
[music] is exactly the opposite of what

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.080
you wanted it to be. You hate your job,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.320
the people around you, your body, and

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.040
most of all, you really hate your life.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.560
You just can't find happiness in

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.640
anything. One night, you're scrolling

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.480
your phone, and you come across a memory

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.400
of you from 10 years ago. And if you're

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.680
thinking, [music]

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.840
"If I could go back to 10 years ago, I

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.560
would change my life completely. If I

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.680
had this opportunity to do that for

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:52.880
myself back then, things would have been

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.240
so different." So, you beg God to take

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.440
you back to 10 years ago so [music] that

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.880
you can start over again and give you

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.840
back that chance. Now, open your eyes.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.960
You are now back 10 years in time. Out

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.400
of everyone who regretted their lives

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.960
and asked for a second chance, God gave

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.200
this to you today, right now. So, tell

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.640
me, what are you going to do from now

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.200
on? Welcome back if you've been here

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.440
before, and if you're new here, my name

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.560
is May, and on this channel, we talk

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.680
about becoming the person you are always

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.720
meant to become, mind, body, spirit, all

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.320
of it. If you're done staying stuck, and

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.480
you're ready to start building a life

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.160
that feels like yours, subscribe and

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.120
join my Stoic Angels community, because

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.760
today we're going deep on the thing that

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:35.520
most people stay stuck in forever, which

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.520
is the inability to let go. The hardest

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.000
part isn't about building a new life,

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.800
but releasing the old one, especially if

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.280
you've been doing this for a while, and

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.440
you have no idea why nothing is

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:49.160
changing. It's because something in you

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:50.840
keeps pulling you back to the nostalgia,

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:52.040
to things that you're familiar with.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.480
It's not because you don't want to

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:55.600
change badly enough, or that you're

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:58.040
weak. Absolutely not. It's because you

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.080
haven't actually properly grieved who

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:03.160
you used to be and you're trying to

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:05.960
build this new thing on top of you still

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:07.920
emotionally living in the past. That's

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.320
why you're stuck. Your present moment

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.240
right now is technically your future

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.360
self's past. That version of you 10

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.280
years from now is looking back at this

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:20.040
exact moment wondering what you did with

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.640
yourself. You really have to feel that.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.240
More importantly, you have to do

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.640
something and act on it. So, here's what

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.200
we're going to do today. I'm going to

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.320
give you a few tools to understand how

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.560
to actually release your old life. By

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:32.480
the end of it, you will have everything

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.600
you need to close this chapter for good

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.560
and finally get your fresh start. I have

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.240
a free resource that I made for you, a

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.520
PDF workbook in the description that

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.000
walks [music] you through everything we

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.760
discuss in the video including the

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.360
exercise we just did and some other

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.920
prompts as well that's not in this

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.680
video. It is completely free for you to

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.320
download and that is my gift to you.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.240
Step one, [music] the framework. Why is

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.080
letting go so hard? Your brain has one

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.000
job, to keep you alive. [music] That's

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.280
it. Your brain also works with your

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.440
nervous system. Keeping you alive means

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:03.000
sticking to what's familiar. It doesn't

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:04.840
care if your relationships are toxic or

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.040
if your coworkers and your classmates

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.040
suck. It only cares [music] that your

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.840
patterns remain predictable.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.000
Psychologists call this the status quo

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.120
bias. People will consistently choose a

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.440
known negative outcome over an unknown

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.160
positive one. We would rather stay in

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.320
the situation we hate than to risk the

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.480
uncertainty of something better. If

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.640
you're replaying old scenarios in your

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.239
head, your brain isn't trying to torture

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.880
you. It's saying, "I have known this

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:32.600
feeling. I have seen it before. I've

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.320
gone through it and I have survived.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.880
Because I've survived, I can do this

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.160
again and I can predict what happens

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.560
next. But that new life though, I don't

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.519
know. I don't have any data or proof

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.357
that it works, which means it's

00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:43.440
dangerous.

00:03:43.357 --> 00:03:44.840
>> [music]

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.320
>> You can't just decide to be different.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:48.840
You have to actually make the leap by

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.959
making whatever seems familiar to you

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.640
feel safe so that your old self can be

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:55.160
let go of." Now, think about one

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.560
specific thing that you know has to go,

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.560
whether it's a relationship or a habit

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:01.920
that's been your comfort blanket for

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:03.200
years. Notice where you feel resistance

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.040
when you think about it and where it

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.760
shows up in your body, whether it's your

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.320
chest, your stomach, your throat. And

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.880
when you feel this resistance, that's

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.920
the part of your body that says, "This

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.240
is mine. Don't take it away from me."

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:17.560
Now, we're going to be working with this

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:19.440
personally. For me, it's been about my

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.480
relationship with food for a really long

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.080
time. So, speaking of building awareness

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.240
around your patterns, one tool that's

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:26.600
been very useful when it comes to

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.440
tracking what I eat is called Lose It.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.360
This is an app that collects insights on

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.520
the positive and negative impacts of

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.680
what I eat and notice patterns that I

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.400
would otherwise completely miss. For

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.080
example, I learned that I naturally eat

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.720
lower calories on days when I have

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:41.520
strawberries.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.919
And if you have iOS, you can even log

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:45.600
your food through Siri. Siri, add an

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:47.400
orange for lunch. That's not all that

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.360
I'm eating, don't worry. Or just scan a

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.400
barcode at the store. It makes logging

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.840
so easy that it becomes effortless. Lose

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.600
It is a free app, but the premium

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.080
features are what I've been using every

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.680
single day. You can try out the premium

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.280
feature for free at my link in the

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.640
description. And thank you to Lose It

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.320
for sponsoring today's video. Step two,

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:06.640
acknowledge there was no other way. Now

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.040
that we understand why our brains hold

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:10.720
on to old narratives so dearly, let's

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.400
talk about the first real step in

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:16.040
letting go. You have to accept what

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.200
happened to you. There was no other way.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:20.360
Whatever it was, every decision that you

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.960
made, whether it's a mistake or not, or

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.440
you felt like you resisted something for

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.883
way too long, or you said the wrong

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.919
thing to the right person,

00:05:24.883 --> 00:05:27.040
>> [music]

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:28.960
>> whatever it is, you made the best

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.320
decision you could with the knowledge

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.160
and the emotional capacity you had at

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.360
the [music] time. I know this may be

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.840
uncomfortable and hard to hear because

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.680
when we think back in hindsight, it

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.360
makes everything look so obvious, right?

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:41.880
Why did I not see that? I should have

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.400
left earlier. I should have done this

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.840
and done that. Your old self wasn't

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:48.440
stupid, either. Your old self was trying

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.440
to keep you safe and did the best that

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.760
they could. Obviously, your old self

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:54.960
also doesn't have as much life

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:57.200
experience as you do now. I spent years

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.280
punishing myself for things that I did,

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:01.120
said, or choices I made when I was

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.440
younger. Trust me, I have made so many

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.840
mistakes and I have failed so many

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.800
times. I failed myself, other people,

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.800
and I would run these scenarios through

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.560
my head. If I had done this differently,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.360
if I had said something different, all

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:15.440
of that mental replaying was only

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.080
keeping me emotionally attached to

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.960
something I couldn't change. I was

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:21.000
running in loops. The truth is, you

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.440
can't go back and fix it. That window of

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.680
opportunity is already gone. So, the

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.880
sooner you can accept this, the sooner

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.080
you can move on with your life and stop

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:32.000
trying to run around in circles. You

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:34.800
know better now and because you know

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.120
better now, you're now available for

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.000
what's next. There was a study published

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.680
in the Journal of Personality and Social

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.280
Psychology where they found that people

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:45.120
who practice self-compassion around

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:47.520
their past showed much higher

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.720
psychological resilience. They're also

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.680
more likely to take positive risks in

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:54.160
the future compared to people who engage

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.040
in chronic self-blame and self-hatred.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.200
And so, in other words, forgiving your

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:00.960
past self doesn't just feel better, it

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.360
literally makes you even more capable of

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.720
building a better life for yourself.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:05.400
Here's what I want you to do now. Think

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.600
of something from your past you've been

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:10.120
carrying some guilt or regret about and

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.760
say to yourself, "I did the best I could

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:15.600
with what I knew. There was no other way

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:17.520
and I'm choosing to move on." That way,

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.400
you are releasing yourself from a prison

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.120
that you don't deserve to be in. And

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:22.960
mind you, you built this prison with

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.919
your own hands. But, the good news is,

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.360
you have the key to unlock that prison

00:07:24.919 --> 00:07:29.280
and free yourself. Step [snorts] three,

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.560
the goodbye ritual. Now, we get to the

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.200
part that I think is the most important

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:35.040
and something that a lot of people don't

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.560
really know they should be doing. You

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:40.000
have to say goodbye. Not just by saying

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:43.000
I'm moving on from this, but by really

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.280
intentionally making it a ceremony so

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:47.120
that you can create significance around

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:49.400
you leaving your old life and grieve

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:51.640
properly because clearly leaving your

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:53.960
old life and building a new one is so

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:55.800
critical for your growth. Grief is not

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:57.560
just for when someone passes away, it's

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:00.240
for every version of yourself you

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:02.440
decided you've already outgrown. I think

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.000
it's very powerful for me to try this

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:07.360
year actually to make my birthday a type

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.120
of funeral as well for my old self.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.240
Everything that has led to this moment

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.160
from the past year I will release on my

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.480
birthday so that I can enter my new

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:17.200
year. I think that's pretty powerful,

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:19.360
too. Grief can also be for every life

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.360
that you have to leave behind so that

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:23.280
you can step out of the pain. If you

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.200
skip the grieving, you actually don't

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:27.000
skip the pain, either. You just carry it

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.800
around with you and all the resentment

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.120
into the next phase of your life [music]

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.039
and that pain and resentment, I promise

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:35.360
you, will show up as some form of

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:37.200
self-sabotage or fear or as a quiet

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:38.560
voice in your head that says, "Who do

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.039
you think you are that you deserve

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:42.640
more?" So, last [music] year I wrote a

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:44.400
very long, elaborate letter to my old

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:47.160
self, a goodbye letter, saying things

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.560
like, "I'm sorry I let you down. I'm

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.120
sorry I didn't stand up for you when I

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:51.640
should have and I'm sorry I gave you

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:53.800
things that made you feel even worse

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:56.360
because I didn't know how to sit with

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:57.761
discomfort and I wanted a way out. I

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:57.800
didn't know a better way.

00:08:57.761 --> 00:09:00.000
>> [music]

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:01.680
>> I'm sorry I let people walk all over you

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.346
and treat you like you were nothing

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:02.360
because

00:09:02.346 --> 00:09:04.280
>> [music]

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.160
>> I believed it, too, and I believed that

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:08.160
I didn't deserve any better." Then, I

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:10.200
stopped apologizing and I started saying

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:11.516
thank you to the old me. Thank you for

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.720
surviving that.

00:09:11.516 --> 00:09:13.200
>> [music]

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:15.600
>> Thank you for getting out of bed even on

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:18.200
days when it felt really hard. Thank you

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.440
for carrying me through every situation

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:22.760
that was difficult and painful that I

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:24.760
didn't deserve and for keeping us alive

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:26.760
long enough to get to this point. It was

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:28.680
such an emotionally charged release for

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:31.000
me, this letter, and I was tearing up

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:33.360
and I was crying because I really felt

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:36.040
that I could let her go now. I remember

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:37.800
I also ended it with, "You did your job.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:39.920
You did what you had to do to get us

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.120
here today. But now I can take it from

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:44.200
here and wherever I'm going, I can't

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.400
take you with me. You get to rest now,

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:48.880
sweetheart. You get to rest while I

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:51.440
carry on. That was such a beautiful

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.880
release and it created so much new

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.840
headspace for me because I gave myself

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:58.120
the permission to lay my old self down

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:00.240
to rest and I felt so much lighter after

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:02.560
that. It was like a burden lifted off of

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.760
me. It felt like a proper funeral which

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:05.160
gave me a lot of closure. It doesn't

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:06.800
even matter whether somebody did

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:08.400
something to you or something that was

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:10.520
completely unfair. At the end of the

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:12.280
day, you're doing this only for

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:14.360
yourself. It's not for anyone else.

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:16.480
Whatever you find it hard to let go of,

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.760
I would have these visualizations, too,

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:20.640
especially for the people in your life

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:23.360
who did me wrong and I see them in my

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.160
mind's eye on the peak of a mountain.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:28.040
Every time they try to come closer to

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:30.320
me, I imagine this invisible force

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:32.520
coming from my aura that pushes them

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:34.520
down that mountain. Goodbye. I don't

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:36.640
want you here in my space anymore. I

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:38.560
deserve this space for myself. So,

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:40.640
here's what I want you to try. Tonight,

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:42.240
whenever you have a quiet moment, grab a

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:43.560
piece of paper and a pen and write a

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.000
letter to that version of you that

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:47.600
you're leaving behind. Tell him or her

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.880
everything. Apologize, show it

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:51.240
gratitude, say goodbye, and tell it what

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:53.280
you deserve from now on and what you

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:55.120
want to let go of. If you feel called

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:57.080
to, you can even burn that piece of

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:59.160
paper and rip it up or bury it in your

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:01.280
backyard. Whatever helps you end that

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:02.920
chapter and feels like closure to you so

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:04.832
you're not holding on to it anymore.

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:06.120
Give it a proper ceremony because it

00:11:04.832 --> 00:11:08.120
[music] really deserves one. That

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:10.400
version of you that kept you surviving

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:12.240
until today deserves a proper send-off.

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:13.920
Step four, don't let nostalgia pull you

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.839
back. A lot of people get tripped up by

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:17.640
the idea of nostalgia. You let go and

00:11:15.839 --> 00:11:19.880
you enter this new you and maybe a few

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:21.800
months in, something happens. You get

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:23.480
bored or lonely or you have a bad day

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:24.880
and your old ways of coping start

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:26.640
calling your name again. You start

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:28.440
singing this familiar song. I mean,

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:30.320
congratulations. You broke the cycle,

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:32.640
but now you have to protect what you

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:34.240
built by not letting go. Boredom or

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:36.680
nostalgia drags you back because

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:38.320
nostalgia is a liar. It makes us see our

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:39.920
past through a rose-tinted lens that's

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:41.680
not really real. It will have you

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:43.600
missing a relationship that made you cry

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:45.280
every night and gave you so much

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:46.960
suffering and it will never show you the

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.080
full picture. It will cherry-pick all

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:51.200
the good moments, play on repeat, and

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:52.520
then edit out all of the bad ones. And

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:54.480
boredom is what happens when your

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:55.760
nervous system has finally calmed down

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.640
and you don't know what to do with

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:59.360
yourself without the chaos. Some people

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:01.800
live their whole lives feeling like

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:03.400
their lives need to be chaotic and have

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:05.520
this constant drama so that they have

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:07.240
something to engage with. When these

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:09.120
people do nervous system work, they're

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:11.560
not used to boredom, especially if you

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:13.200
grew up in a dysfunctional family or if

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:15.720
the last chapter that you closed was

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:17.440
full of emotional highs and lows and a

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:18.920
lot of drama. Peace is going to feel

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:21.200
boring to you. I don't know if you know

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:23.040
people like this, but they might even

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:24.840
subconsciously create problems just to

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:26.880
feel something because their body is so

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:28.040
used to being intense. Now, I have

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:29.520
people like that in my life and I

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:31.000
realized after a while I cannot help

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.720
these people. They have to learn how to

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:35.160
help themselves. Also, nobody is

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:36.440
responsible for anyone else's life. So,

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.960
realistically speaking, this is

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:40.240
something that everyone has to learn to

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:41.760
do on your own. If that sounds like you,

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:43.839
when you notice the pull to go back,

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:46.040
don't judge yourself and just see it and

00:12:43.839 --> 00:12:48.400
observe it. When you get to observe

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:50.680
something, you can choose differently.

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:52.640
Dr. Tara Brach talks about this as the

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:55.680
practice of rain, which stands for

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:57.400
recognize, allow, investigate, nurture.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:00.360
When the urge to slip back hits you,

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:03.160
recognize what's happening, allow it to

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:05.200
exist without acting on it, investigate

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:07.280
where this feeling lives in your body

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:09.360
and what it really needs, and then

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:11.440
nurture yourself the way you're meant to

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:12.960
be nurtured, which is probably not going

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:14.600
back to the old pattern. Everything that

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:17.040
you lost, I promise you, will be

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:19.440
replaced with something even better, but

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:21.560
this will only happen if you let the

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:24.000
replacement happen and it can't arrive

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:25.000
if you keep going back to check on the

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.880
things that [music] you buried, like

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:29.200
looking at old messages, looking at your

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:32.000
ex Instagram stories? No, we can't go

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:33.640
back. Step five, enter the new you.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.280
We've talked plenty about why we hold on

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:37.240
to things and about accepting there is

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:39.080
no other way back. We said our goodbyes

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:40.360
and we built awareness around nostalgia.

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:43.040
Now, let's talk about what you're

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:45.120
stepping into, your new timeline. In

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:46.640
your own time, close your eyes and

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.840
imagine the best possible version of

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:50.880
you, only for yourself. When you close

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:52.600
your eyes and let yourself drift off and

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:55.040
drift away, dream without any kind of

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:56.920
limitation, that person is truly who you

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:59.200
are and what you deserve to be. They

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:01.560
exist underneath all of these layers of

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:04.080
protection, survival, and coping

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:06.520
mechanisms built for environments that

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:08.560
you were never meant to stay in forever,

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:10.840
especially if you have removed yourself

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:13.000
from these environments now. Imagining

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:15.280
that person is the easy part. The harder

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:16.640
part is letting go of every part of you

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:18.640
that doesn't believe in it. Because some

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:21.280
parts will resist and say, "Well, people

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:23.200
like me don't deserve to have that." Or,

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:25.520
"I've already messed up too badly. I am

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:27.160
evil." Or, "What if I fail and I prove

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:28.760
that I really just don't deserve this at

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:30.960
all?" These negative thoughts are not

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:32.680
your enemies. They're actually your

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:34.960
bodyguards, protecting you from

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:37.240
disappointment, but you don't need them

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:38.920
anymore. You can thank them and release

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:40.839
them now, just like how you release the

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:42.400
old version of yourself. I also written

00:14:40.839 --> 00:14:44.200
another letter to these old guards,

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:45.880
thanking them for their service and for

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:47.839
how they have done their best to protect

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:49.760
me and not want to see me suffer or be

00:14:47.839 --> 00:14:51.080
disappointed. Let them go, too. Another

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:52.720
thing you can do is when you wake up in

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:54.680
the morning, before you do anything

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:56.920
else, put your hand on your heart and

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:58.600
say, "I am becoming who I've always

00:14:56.920 --> 00:15:00.680
been." Then ask yourself, "What would

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:02.800
this person do today, this new version

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:04.600
of me?" And just do that one thing that

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.320
aligns with the answer. If you continue

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:08.240
to do it, this is how your timeline can

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:10.440
change. Every decision that you make

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:12.480
after that is actively being made in

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:14.839
your best interest. Now, I want to hear

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:17.120
from you, so drop a comment and say, "I

00:15:14.839 --> 00:15:18.680
am stepping into my best timeline." And

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:20.120
now everyone will read your comment,

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.320
your comment will affirm this with you,

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:24.000
and so will I. I see your comments as

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:25.720
well and there's something so powerful

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.040
about just declaring it and putting it

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:28.560
out there in the world. If you want to

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.160
take this even deeper, I have a free

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.960
resource that I made for you. It's my

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:33.360
gift, a free PDF guide in the

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:35.520
description that walks you through the

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.880
goodbye letter exercise step-by-step,

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:38.280
including prompts for what to write if

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:39.720
you're not sure where to start. And this

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:41.160
is something that I have made especially

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:42.520
for this video. It is free because I

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.920
know how hard it is to just sit with a

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:45.600
blank page and remember things after

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:47.280
watching a YouTube video. So, go

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:48.880
download that if you haven't already and

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:51.320
realize you don't need a time machine or

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:52.960
any kind of regret or wasted time in

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:55.120
order to make this decision now. All you

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:56.760
have to do is live your present moment

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:58.360
like your future self looking into the

00:15:56.760 --> 00:16:00.600
past and stop living like your actual

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:02.680
past is still happening to you. It's no

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:04.680
longer here with us in this room today.

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:06.360
Your new life is trying to reach you.

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:08.200
So, your only job from now is to stop

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:09.560
blocking it with the old one. I believe

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:11.200
in you, angel. I believe in the new

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:12.480
version of you that's emerging, even if

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:14.080
it feels uncertain and they don't know

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:15.400
what we're doing. I think we all don't.

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:17.200
Nobody knows what the future holds for

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:19.280
us and that's okay. We just have to keep

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:20.920
moving forward. I make so many videos

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:22.200
and there's so much more coming about

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:23.880
changing your life, welcoming your

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.000
future self. So, subscribe if you

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:26.800
haven't already and don't forget I

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:30.120
believe in you, angel, and I love you. I

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:30.120
will see you in the next video.

